    "lawCode": "Amd §1220-b, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5643\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to unlawful\nsolicitation of ground transportation services at an airport\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nThis legislation would increase the penalty for unlawful solicitation\nof ground transportation services at airports to a class B misdemeanor\nfor the first offense; punishable by a fine of not less than five\nhundred dollars nor more than one thousand two hundred fifty dollars,\nor by imprisonment of not more than ninety days or both such fine and\nimprisonment\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nThis bill would amend the penalty provision of section 1220-b of the\nVehicle and Traffic Law, which prohibits the unlawful solicitation of\nground transportation services at airports. The present penalty\nprovision for this offense sets forth a graduated schedule of fines\nfor succeeding convictions. This bill would instead make this offense\npunishable as a class B misdemeanor.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nSection 1220-b of the Vehicle and Traffic Law makes it a crime for any\ndriver, for a price and without authorization or prearrangement, to\noffer ground transportation at an airport. Nevertheless, illegal\nhustling continues to be a problem at New York City airports.\nPassengers, often unfamiliar with ground transportation options, are\nsolicited by illegal drivers upon debarking at the terminal. Section\n1220-b, though it prohibits this practice, punishes initial offenses\nwith relatively small fines which are easily accepted as a cost of\ndoing business. In the absence of an effective penalty, the prohibited\npractice flourishes.\n\nThis bill would close this enforcement loophole by making unlawful\nsolicitation of ground transportation services at airports, beginning\nwith the first offense, a class B misdemeanor. with an effective\npenalty, enforcement authorities will be able to address the problem\npresented by illegal and unscrupulous drivers at airports.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2007-08: A8868B passed assembly, delivered to senate and referred to\nRules. 2009: A4552 referred to Transportation.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
